# Write Verification Observers

These analysis tools implement the observation-only first two phases of MCP
write verification. They do not change registered tools, registered-tool
response schemas or success semantics, C++ commands, save behavior, or runtime
policy.

## Phase 1: Advisory Inventory

Generate a heuristic inventory of all registered tools:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\mcp_write_tool_inventory.py check
```

The JSON report is written under
`MCPGameProject/Saved/MCP/WriteVerification` by default. It classifies tools as
`read`, `write`, `destructive`, or `lifecycle`, assigns a review priority, and
records wrapper-level verification signals. Warnings are advisory and never
disable a tool or fail the command.

The inventory is intentionally conservative. A missing Python signal does not
prove that the corresponding C++ handler lacks a postcondition, and every P0
or P1 item requires manual review before enforcement is considered. Tools with
no reliable static classification signal are assigned P1 even when their
provisional classification is `read`; low confidence never qualifies for P2.
Read-oriented names do not override known conditional mutation parameters such
as `create_if_missing`, `write_report`, or `output_path`; those tools remain in
the write review queue even when the mutating option defaults to false. Wrapper
source is normalized by its function-level indentation before AST inspection.
If parsing still fails, `bridge_commands` stays empty and the report records a
parse error and warning instead of treating arbitrary string literals as
commands.

## Phase 2: Independent Observer

Validate the observer contract without connecting to Unreal:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\run_mcp_write_postcondition_observer.py check
```

Observe an existing Niagara Custom HLSL smoke report without mutating assets:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\run_mcp_write_postcondition_observer.py observe-niagara `
  --report .\MCPGameProject\Saved\MCP\NiagaraReviews\<run-id>\scratch_pad_graph_authoring_report.json
```

Run the single live observer only while the UE 5.6.1 editor bridge is already
running:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\run_mcp_write_postcondition_observer.py live
```

The live observer delegates one existing planner-driven Blueprint smoke under
`/Game/_MCP_Temp/WriteVerificationObserver`. It requests no durable save,
checks readback and compile evidence, scans new log errors, and requires the
temporary Blueprint to be deleted by the existing smoke cleanup. Missing log,
leftover, structural-readback, compile, cleanup, or no-save evidence fails
closed rather than being interpreted as an empty successful result.
`durable_save_attempted` is tri-state: `true` means an attempt was reported,
`false` requires complete no-save evidence, and `null` means the evidence was
incomplete. Duplicate compile fields at the execution and validation levels
must agree in both type and value. A live run removes any pre-existing delegated
JSON for the same run ID before launch, so a zero-exit subprocess must create
fresh evidence to pass. Default run IDs include microseconds plus a random UUID
suffix, and an exclusive lock prevents concurrent observers from sharing an
explicit run ID. A lock conflict fails into a separate report directory without
touching the active run, including when the conflicting request has an invalid
timeout. Lock files carry a per-invocation ownership marker so exception and
interruption cleanup can remove only the current invocation's lock. A lock
release failure changes the top-level report and exit code to failure instead
of returning success with a stale lock. Every `safe_executions` item must be an
object; malformed mixed lists fail closed. Blueprint source reports must identify
themselves as `planner_driven_bp_authoring_smoke`, and a top-level live-gate
error is rejected even when the remaining success fields look valid.

The delegated live smoke has a 600-second process timeout by default. Override
it with `--timeout-seconds` when needed. Timeout and malformed delegated report
paths produce a structured failed observer report; they never count as a
successful observation. Malformed JSON includes invalid UTF-8 and JSON syntax.
A timeout reports that mutation may have occurred and that the temporary scope
needs a manual cleanup check. Explicit `--run-id` values are restricted to 1-64
ASCII characters, must start with a letter or digit, and may otherwise contain
only letters, digits, underscores, or hyphens, so they cannot escape the
observer output or `/Game/_MCP_Temp` scope.

Exit code `2` means the editor bridge was unavailable and the live observer
was safely skipped. A skipped JSON report has `success=false` and
`verification_performed=false`, so report-only consumers cannot mistake the
skip for live evidence. Pass `--require-live` only when an unavailable bridge
must be treated as a normal command failure instead of a skip.

For an existing Niagara report, the observer distinguishes
`asset_absent_now` from `cleanup_verified`. Current file absence alone is only
state evidence; `cleanup_verified=true` additionally requires explicit cleanup
execution evidence in the source report. It also verifies the post-compile
error count and derives the expected output pin from the recorded admission
request before comparing it with graph readback. The observer performs no
Niagara write; the source smoke report may describe saved temporary assets, and
that fact is surfaced separately as `source_report_indicates_save`. Niagara
asset absence is explicitly filesystem-only; in-memory editor state is not
claimed by offline observation. Each Custom HLSL replacement must retain and
verify non-equal chained ChangeIds, changed/stable/applied counts for every
affected script, zero affected compile errors, and exact expected-versus-actual
HLSL readback. Older reports that do not contain these raw fields must be rerun;
source success booleans alone are not accepted. Replacement and graph-node
lists reject non-object entries, and the graph-readback node GUID must match the
GUID used by the complete replacement chain.

## Boundaries

These observers do not enforce write postconditions. Durable save/reopen,
Undo enforcement, automatic live Niagara deletion, mutation locks, and changes
to existing `success` behavior remain out of scope until separately approved.
